# Personal commit blocklist — TEMPLATE.
#
# Copy this to `.commit-blocklist` (which is gitignored) and put your own
# sensitive strings there. A staged change that ADDS any entry blocks the
# commit. Keep the real values only in `.commit-blocklist`, never in this
# tracked template.
#
# Format:
#   - Blank lines and lines starting with `#` are ignored.
#   - An entry matches as a case-insensitive literal substring
#     (dots in a domain or IP are literal, not regex).
#   - Prefix an entry with `re:` for a case-insensitive regex — use this for
#     word-bounded names so a short name doesn't over-match, e.g.
#         re:\bJane Doe\b
#
# Behavior:
#   - No `.commit-blocklist` file => the guard is a silent no-op (opt-in).
#   - A malformed list (bad regex) => the commit fails closed.
#   - The guard reports file:line and the blocklist ENTRY NUMBER, never the
#     matched value — so it can't re-leak what you're blocking. Look the entry
#     up with:  sed -n '<N>p' .commit-blocklist
#   - `git commit --no-verify` bypasses it (it's convenience, not enforcement).
#
# Example entries (illustrative — delete, and add your own in .commit-blocklist):
#   example.internal.host
#   203.0.113.10
#   re:\bYour Name\b
